**Definition 8.2 (exact per-row structural stencil).** At row $j$, use
SSA Definition 8.1's ordered cell-role list $\mathcal C_j$, append-site
list $\mathcal A_j$, old-graph head-slot walks
$\mathcal J_{j,\rm old}$ and actual routing words. Repeated routes and
roles are charged again. Add a fresh finite-slot root walk and its two
child-slot probes for each fresh bit; call that list $\mathcal Z_j$.
The root-to-register path has length $d_r$. Full inspection of the
pre-row register tree costs $3(2q-1+4c_{j-1})-1$. Thus the exact probe
prescription is

$$
\begin{aligned}
p_j={}&d_r+6q+12c_{j-1}-4
 +\sum_{x\in\mathcal C_j}(|\pi_x|+140)\\
 &+\sum_{z\in\mathcal A_j}(|\pi_z|+2)
 +\sum_{w\in\mathcal J_{j,\rm old}}|w|
 +\sum_{z\in\mathcal Z_j}(|\pi_z|+2).
\end{aligned}
\tag{CSA.11}
$$

These are the supplier's register/cell/append/head inspections in that
order, then the fresh-slot checks. New-vertex slot initialization is an
assignment rather than an old-graph probe. Root-to-cell routes use the
maintained head links and fixed adjacent-cell walks, never a free Seek
to an unacquired numeric address.

Let $e_j$ be the number of actual core-plus-receipt cell appends,
$s_j$ the number of fresh finite bits, $t_j$ the number of designated
already-existing bit targets after native alias combination, $r_j$ the
number of retargeted head links and $a_j$ the number of first activations.
Include finite native control assignments in $t_j$, but exclude the new
bit constructed in $s_j$. Define

$$
\begin{aligned}
v_j&=48e_j+4s_j,\\
w_j&=4t_j+2e_j+2s_j+3r_j+2a_j+7v_j,\\
g_j&=1+p_j+w_j+v_j.
\end{aligned}
\tag{CSA.12}
$$

The $2s_j+28s_j$ constructor assignments agree with Definition 3.3's
30, without adding another four existing-bit assignments. The receipt
is combined with the core in this single commitment. No-op designated
writes remain charged. A standalone fresh-bit core, excluding native
control/receipt, has four new vertices and 30 assignments; it is not an
extra separately committed event in this execution.

